==Freedom of panorama== <!--T:54-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|FOP|Japan}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:55-->
* for artistic works: {{NotMark}} {{tlx|NoFoP-Japan}} except in cases governed by Article 46.
* for buildings only: {{OkMark}} {{tlx|FoP-Japan}}

<!--T:56-->
According to the ''Copyright Act (Act No. 48 of May 6, 1970, amended up to January 1, 2022/April 1, 2026)'':
</translate>
*<translate><!--T:90--> It is permissible to exploit an artistic work the original copy of which is permanently installed in an outdoor location as provided for in paragraph (2) of the preceding Article or an architectural work, in any way whatsoever except the following:</translate>
::<translate><!--T:91--> (i) producing additional copies of a sculpture or making those additional copies of the sculpture available to the public by transferring them;</translate>
::<translate><!--T:92--> (ii) reproducing an architectural work by means of construction, or making copies of an architectural work so reproduced available to the public by transferring them;</translate>
::<translate><!--T:93--> (iii) reproducing a work in order to permanently install it in an outdoor location as provided for in paragraph (2) of the preceding Article;</translate>
::<translate><!--T:94--> (iv) reproducing an artistic work for the purpose of selling copies of it, or selling those copies.{{sref|48/1970-2022-2026 Art. 46}}</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:95-->
The outdoor locations sppecified in the preceding article are "on a street, at a park, or in any other outdoor location accessible to the public, or on the outer wall of a building or other place easily seen by the public."{{sref|48/1970-2022-2026 Art. 45(2)}}

<!--T:57-->
Architectural works located in such places, including buildings, gardens, bridges and towers ([https://monolith.law/en/general-corporate/building-image-use-illegal source]), may be photographed and the photos may be reproduced for any purposes. The restriction on "selling" copies only applies to the "artistic works." However, non-commercial licenses {{pg|<tvar name="1">Commons:Licensing</tvar>|are not free enough for Commons}}. Some buildings like the {{wp-Tower of the Sun|Tower of the Sun}} can be regarded as artistic works per [[<tvar name="2">Commons talk:Freedom of panorama/Archive 1#Hideyuki_Murata</tvar>|discussion]].

<!--T:81-->
Regarding buildings, a [http://www.courts.go.jp/app/files/hanrei_jp/737/010737_hanrei.pdf 2003 ruling by the Osaka District Court] states that in order for a building to be copyrighted, it "must have creativity in aesthetic expression in light of the definition of works stipulated in Article 2, Paragraph 1, Item 1 of the same Act." This means ordinary-looking buildings are not eligible for copyright protection as "architectural works".

<!--T:83-->
For more information, refer to:
* [<tvar name="url">http://www.murata-law.jp/kigyou/cs-kenchiku.html</tvar> 著作権法 – 建築に関する著作物について (Copyright Law – About Architectural Works)], by 村田法律事務所 (Murata Law Office) ([<tvar name="url2">https://web.archive.org/web/20090901103724/http://www.murata-law.jp/kigyou/cs-kenchiku.html</tvar> archived copy]) — in Japanese
</translate>

;<translate><!--T:96--> Mandatory attribution even in derivative works, except in the case of anonymous works</translate>
*<translate><!--T:97--> In a case set forth in one of the following items, the source of the work must be clearly indicated in the manner and to the extent considered reasonable, commensurate with the circumstances of its reproduction or exploitation:... a work is exploited other than by its reproduction pursuant to the provisions of Article 32 or Article 42, or is exploited pursuant to the provisions of Article 35, paragraph (1); Article 36, paragraph (1); Article 38, paragraph (1); Article 41; Article 41-2, paragraph (2); Article 42-2, paragraph (2); '''Article 46'''; or Article 47-5, paragraph (1), and it is common practice to indicate the source.{{sref|48/1970-2026 Art. 48(1)(iii)}}</translate>
*<translate><!--T:98--> In a source indication as referred to in the preceding paragraph, the name of the author that appears on the work must be cited, except if the author's name is clear from the source indication or if the work is anonymous.{{sref|48/1970-2026 Art. 48(2)}}</translate>
*<translate><!--T:99--> If a work is exploited as provided in one of the following items, the source of the original work underlying the derivative work provided for in that item must be indicated pursuant to the provisions of the preceding two paragraphs:... a derivative work created pursuant to the provisions of Article 40, paragraph (1); '''Article 46'''; or Article 47-5, paragraph (1) is exploited pursuant to those provisions.{{sref|48/1970-2026 Art. 48(3)(i)}}</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:58-->
;Notes:
*''Article 46:'' "Partial Amendment of Act No. 77 of 1999." ''Article 48:'' "Partial Amendment of Act No. 62 of 1985; Act No. 56 of 2000; Act No. 85 of 2003; Act No. 121 of 2006; Act No. 53 of 2009; Act No. 30 of 2018; Act No. 39 of 2018; and Act No. 33 of 2023)."
*According to the copyright law, Japan has a copyright duration of 70 years after the death of the author (ie. creator/designer) or following "the death of the last surviving co-author in the case of a joint work." Henceforth, the author's works shall become copyright free and enter the public domain.{{sref|48/1970-2022-2026 Art. 51}}
</translate>

